WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.010
Any team can build an agent in an afternoon now.

2
00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:03.950
What holds a company back is managing it.

3
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.730
In 2026, we learned to build agents.

4
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.150
2027 is the year to learn to manage them.

5
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.830
And putting an agent into an old process just delivers the same mistake faster.

6
00:00:13.139 --> 00:00:13.209
process just delivers the same mistake faster.

7
00:00:13.209 --> 00:00:14.859
Eight competencies, ten modules,

8
00:00:14.859 --> 00:00:16.019
open courses and projects,

9
00:00:16.019 --> 00:00:16.679
all free.

10
00:00:16.679 --> 00:00:17.679
In this video,

11
00:00:17.679 --> 00:00:19.419
I’ll show you what changes in management,

12
00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:20.539
what each course teaches,

13
00:00:20.539 --> 00:00:21.499
what the projects are,

14
00:00:21.499 --> 00:00:22.559
and how to use them in your

15
00:00:22.559 --> 00:00:23.579
work, your team,

16
00:00:23.579 --> 00:00:24.459
and your company.

17
00:00:24.459 --> 00:00:25.379
And by the end,

18
00:00:25.379 --> 00:00:26.819
you’ll have your first exercise for this

19
00:00:26.819 --> 00:00:28.639
week: take apart one process.

20
00:00:28.639 --> 00:00:30.739
Management has gained a third thing to

21
00:00:30.739 --> 00:00:31.139
manage.

22
00:00:31.139 --> 00:00:32.919
From 1980 to 2000,

23
00:00:32.919 --> 00:00:34.039
it was people management:

24
00:00:34.039 --> 00:00:36.119
roles, responsibilities, boundaries,

25
00:00:36.119 --> 00:00:36.959
and reviews.

26
00:00:36.959 --> 00:00:38.579
From 2000 to 2020,

27
00:00:38.579 --> 00:00:39.919
it was process management.

28
00:00:39.919 --> 00:00:42.079
Then came digital transformation and automation.

29
00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:43.919
In 2025 and 2026,

30
00:00:43.919 --> 00:00:44.899
it was building agents:

31
00:00:44.899 --> 00:00:46.479
prompts, skills, context,

32
00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:47.719
tools, and memory.

33
00:00:47.719 --> 00:00:50.399
In 2027, a company coordinates people,

34
00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:52.499
agents, models, processes,

35
00:00:52.499 --> 00:00:54.459
and compute budgets all at once.

36
00:00:54.459 --> 00:00:56.119
Building agents is easy now.

37
00:00:56.119 --> 00:00:57.799
What holds us back is what comes

38
00:00:57.799 --> 00:00:59.819
next: who’s accountable for an agent,

39
00:00:59.819 --> 00:01:01.019
what it can do on its own,

40
00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:02.639
how we measure whether it’s doing a

41
00:01:02.639 --> 00:01:04.219
good job, and how much each result

42
00:01:04.219 --> 00:01:04.999
costs.

43
00:01:04.999 --> 00:01:06.859
Managing agents is a lot like old-fashioned

44
00:01:06.859 --> 00:01:08.839
people management, just with more measurement.

45
00:01:08.839 --> 00:01:09.779
The words change.

46
00:01:09.779 --> 00:01:10.719
The logic doesn’t.

47
00:01:10.719 --> 00:01:11.699
For an employee,

48
00:01:11.699 --> 00:01:12.599
there was a job title,

49
00:01:12.599 --> 00:01:13.519
responsibilities,

50
00:01:13.519 --> 00:01:15.359
tools, boundaries, and a review.

51
00:01:15.359 --> 00:01:16.259
For an agent:

52
00:01:16.259 --> 00:01:17.759
a role, objective,

53
00:01:17.759 --> 00:01:19.919
context, tools, autonomy,

54
00:01:19.919 --> 00:01:21.199
and evaluation.

55
00:01:21.199 --> 00:01:22.299
The same items,

56
00:01:22.299 --> 00:01:23.179
plus two more:

57
00:01:23.179 --> 00:01:25.999
explicit context and graduated autonomy.

58
00:01:25.999 --> 00:01:27.619
Here’s what that looks like in practice.

59
00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:29.359
In accounting, the person who posts an

60
00:01:29.359 --> 00:01:30.379
entry can’t approve it:

61
00:01:30.379 --> 00:01:31.559
the agent goes right up to the

62
00:01:31.559 --> 00:01:33.139
signature step, but never beyond it.

63
00:01:33.139 --> 00:01:33.919
In legal work,

64
00:01:33.919 --> 00:01:34.779
the agent prepares;

65
00:01:34.779 --> 00:01:36.299
the lawyer decides and signs.

66
00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:38.419
In healthcare, some decisions are outside the

67
00:01:38.419 --> 00:01:40.199
agent’s authority, and that boundary is set

68
00:01:40.199 --> 00:01:41.359
before it goes live.

69
00:01:41.359 --> 00:01:42.679
Now for the hardest message:

70
00:01:42.679 --> 00:01:44.239
don’t use agents to run old processes

71
00:01:44.239 --> 00:01:44.919
faster.

72
00:01:44.919 --> 00:01:46.739
Redesign the company for their speed.

73
00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:48.559
Here’s the question that changes everything:

74
00:01:48.559 --> 00:01:49.899
if people, agents,

75
00:01:49.899 --> 00:01:51.539
and automation were all available today,

76
00:01:51.539 --> 00:01:53.359
would this process still exist in this

77
00:01:53.359 --> 00:01:53.939
form?

78
00:01:53.939 --> 00:01:54.559
Three rules.

79
00:01:54.559 --> 00:01:55.839
Don’t automate a bad process.

80
00:01:55.839 --> 00:01:58.039
First, take apart whatever exists just out

81
00:01:58.039 --> 00:01:58.559
of habit.

82
00:01:58.559 --> 00:02:00.639
People lead; agents execute.

83
00:02:00.639 --> 00:02:02.019
Decide, step by step,

84
00:02:02.019 --> 00:02:02.839
who does what.

85
00:02:02.839 --> 00:02:04.919
And promoting and rolling back are steps,

86
00:02:04.919 --> 00:02:05.739
not accidents.

87
00:02:05.739 --> 00:02:07.199
A process has versions,

88
00:02:07.199 --> 00:02:08.399
just like software.

89
00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:10.139
That’s LOOP-R: run,

90
00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:12.119
observe, critique, propose,

91
00:02:12.119 --> 00:02:14.239
experiment, validate, promote,

92
00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:15.019
and repeat.

93
00:02:15.019 --> 00:02:16.259
There’s culture, too.

94
00:02:16.259 --> 00:02:17.999
Anthropic builds two cultures at the same

95
00:02:17.999 --> 00:02:19.639
time: one for people and one for

96
00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:20.799
AI itself.

97
00:02:20.799 --> 00:02:22.339
In January 2026,

98
00:02:22.339 --> 00:02:24.059
it published a constitution for Claude,

99
00:02:24.059 --> 00:02:25.439
with values, priorities,

100
00:02:25.439 --> 00:02:27.119
and the reasons behind each one.

101
00:02:27.119 --> 00:02:28.039
At your company,

102
00:02:28.039 --> 00:02:29.639
that becomes three practices.

103
00:02:29.639 --> 00:02:31.559
Write a constitution for each agent:

104
00:02:31.559 --> 00:02:33.359
one page covering its mission,

105
00:02:33.359 --> 00:02:35.119
limits, and when to escalate.

106
00:02:35.119 --> 00:02:36.239
Set green, yellow,

107
00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:36.939
and red limits:

108
00:02:36.939 --> 00:02:38.319
what it handles on its own,

109
00:02:38.319 --> 00:02:40.159
what it prepares for someone to approve,

110
00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:41.419
and what it sends straight to a

111
00:02:41.419 --> 00:02:41.859
manager.

112
00:02:41.859 --> 00:02:43.459
And measure it with test cases:

113
00:02:43.459 --> 00:02:45.259
if a review doesn’t improve its score

114
00:02:45.259 --> 00:02:45.979
on the same cases,

115
00:02:45.979 --> 00:02:46.939
the agent hasn’t improved.

116
00:02:46.939 --> 00:02:48.899
To measure where you’re starting from,

117
00:02:48.899 --> 00:02:50.839
this section lays out the AI manager’s

118
00:02:50.839 --> 00:02:52.579
eight competencies, in the order you use

119
00:02:52.579 --> 00:02:53.019
them.

120
00:02:53.019 --> 00:02:54.599
The first four decide what the agent

121
00:02:54.599 --> 00:02:55.339
will do.

122
00:02:55.339 --> 00:02:56.979
Strategy: what result do we want,

123
00:02:56.979 --> 00:02:58.159
before choosing a tool?

124
00:02:58.159 --> 00:03:01.339
Work architecture: should this process still exist?

125
00:03:01.339 --> 00:03:03.039
Delegation: exactly what am I giving the

126
00:03:03.039 --> 00:03:04.359
agent, with an objective,

127
00:03:04.359 --> 00:03:06.179
context, tools, deadline,

128
00:03:06.179 --> 00:03:07.259
and proof of completion?

129
00:03:07.259 --> 00:03:09.339
And orchestration: one agent or several,

130
00:03:09.339 --> 00:03:10.539
in sequence or in parallel,

131
00:03:10.539 --> 00:03:12.359
with a supervisor or a reviewer?

132
00:03:12.499 --> 00:03:13.999
The last four decide whether it keeps

133
00:03:13.999 --> 00:03:15.219
doing the work.

134
00:03:15.219 --> 00:03:16.739
Governance: what it does on its own,

135
00:03:16.739 --> 00:03:17.619
what it confirms,

136
00:03:17.619 --> 00:03:19.079
and what it never does.

137
00:03:19.079 --> 00:03:20.939
Economics: what it costs to produce the

138
00:03:20.939 --> 00:03:22.659
result, not what one call costs.

139
00:03:22.659 --> 00:03:23.999
A cheap agent that makes a mistake

140
00:03:23.999 --> 00:03:26.059
and needs two tries costs twice as

141
00:03:26.059 --> 00:03:27.319
much as the rate card says.

142
00:03:27.319 --> 00:03:28.639
That’s a manager’s calculation,

143
00:03:28.639 --> 00:03:30.399
not a programmer’s.

144
00:03:30.439 --> 00:03:31.359
Evaluation:

145
00:03:31.359 --> 00:03:32.419
is it good,

146
00:03:32.419 --> 00:03:34.039
compared with what?

147
00:03:34.039 --> 00:03:35.699
Quality, time, cost,

148
00:03:35.699 --> 00:03:37.639
rework, and how much it did without

149
00:03:37.639 --> 00:03:38.199
stopping.

150
00:03:38.199 --> 00:03:40.039
And evolution: what did we learn from

151
00:03:40.039 --> 00:03:41.059
this run?

